  set:  # 5d40
    title: "python.yaml's stdlib_modules is a 3.12 snapshot: distutils/imp/asyncore/smtpd/binhex (stdlib on 3.10/3.11) are labelled third_party; make the stdlib line the union over supported Pythons (ADR-0041 §3)"  # 5d40
    description: "Found 2026-10-03 while fixing WI-jojuz (its test-side twin).\n\nWHAT. io_primitives/python.yaml's `stdlib_modules` is generated from ONE interpreter (`scripts/refresh-stdlib-modules python --version 3.12`). So IoBoundaryCatalog.is_stdlib_module answers \"stdlib on 3.12\", while analyzed code may target any Python, and hypergumbo supports 3.10-3.13. Measured on dev (load_catalog('python').is_stdlib_module):\n  False: distutils, distutils.spawn, imp, asyncore, smtpd, binhex  (stdlib on 3.10/3.11)\n  True:  tomllib (3.11+); cgi, telnetlib (removed in 3.13, present only because the snapshot is 3.12)\nVersion windows come from Python/stdlib_module_names.h on the CPython 3.10-3.13 branches.\n\nGOVERNING RULING: ADR-0041 §3.\n- Single source: one stdlib catalogue per language, one writer (the generator), feeding every recognition consumer.\n- Ecosystem axis: `ecosystem` = shipped with the language runtime (stdlib) vs fetched from a registry (third_party). `distutils` IS shipped with the 3.10/3.11 runtime, so labelling it third_party contradicts the axis definition.\n- \"Not a consumer: the io-boundary closed-world gates (INV-buzab)\" is normative. Gates ask module_io_is_enumerated; they never ask is_stdlib_module. Widening the list cannot loosen a verdict. (This item's original \"needs an owner ruling on the gate direction\" framing was wrong: that question was already settled. The owner approved the fix on 2026-10-03.)\n\nCONSUMERS (all labelling or ordering): (1) the ecosystem stamp on external boundary nodes and the supply_chain_summary counts (cli._make_ecosystem_classifier); (2) the py_deps manifest carve-out; (3) the third-party-first ordering of verify-claims' uncatalogued-module caveat. WI-bifih's import-resolution wiring was overtaken by events (its delivery was submodule ecosystem stamping).\n\nAPPROVED FIX (owner, 2026-10-03):\n- The stdlib line = the UNION over the supported interpreters. One shared table in core (supported versions + per-module version windows) feeds the generator and the WI-jojuz scope gate.\n- stdlib_provenance declares `supported_versions`, which the generator checks.\n- python.yaml is regenerated.\n- ADR-0016 (\"the actual interpreter\") and the io_boundary docstring are reworded.\n- Acceptance: a distutils.spawn fixture's ecosystem label moves third_party -> stdlib while its verify-claims verdict is UNCHANGED (still withheld; distutils is unaudited).\nKnown cost: on 3.12+, PyPI backports (standard-telnetlib, standard-asynchat, ...) providing those import names are dropped by the py_deps carve-out. That is already true today for cgi/telnetlib."  # 5d40
